; Intent 9: "a prefix match that only matches at a delimiter".
;
; The other POSITIVE CONTROL, and the one that also shows --implies
; DISCRIMINATING: --spec matched str-prefix on the verbatim first law, and proof
; refutes it on the second — a longer value does match a bare prefix, which is
; the defect this intent was about.
;
; ARGUMENT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING HERE. Written the other way round — asking
; whether (want = s+letter) matches (got = s) — the law is true of str-prefix
; TOO and discriminates nothing, which is how a first draft of this file
; "reproduced" the hits while losing the refutation that is the point.
(defn wanted [] [(want Str) (got Str)] Bool false
  (prop identical-is-a-match [(s Str)]
    (wanted s s))
  (prop an-extra-letter-breaks-the-match [(s Str)]
    (not (wanted s (str-append s (SCons 97 SNil))))))
